SEOUL (Reuters) - Following a direct request from US President Donald Trump to South Korean President Moon Jae-in in March, South Korea decided to package a number of coronavirus (COVID-19) test kits. 600,000 copies to be shipped to the United States on Tuesday in the first phase. This is according to the release by CNA on Monday evening, April 13, 2020.
Donald Trump's request was made on March 25 in a telephone conversation with South Korean President Moon Jae-in, in which Trump also made some promises to South Korea, such as allowing a Korean company. Some Southerners have legal licenses to trade with the United States.
The COVID-19 test kit will be shipped out of South Korea at 10:30 pm on Tuesday via a US Federal Emergency Management Agency cargo plane. The first phase of testing equipment is under US government funding, while the second phase will have another 150,000 units, which will be completed soon.
