Understand the law: undivided ownership
To better understand what undivided ownership is? What are the rights and obligations of undivided property owners? Can undivided property be classified?
Therefore, the Legal Awareness Program of Fresh News in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation would like to make the following interpretations related to the topic of "undivided ownership" to the public:
In managing the use of undivided property in accordance with the law, each owner has the right to grant or utilize his undivided share without the consent of the other undivided owner, and the debt of the undivided owner. This can also seize that portion.
Each owner of an undivided object shall be responsible for the payment of tax administration costs in proportion to his or her share, and in the event that any owner has paid for maintenance costs, management or other obligations in excess of his / her obligations. Pay The owner may make a claim that the other owner pays the excess in proportion to his respective share.
On the other hand, the rights of the above claim can also be applied to those who have inherited other undivided shares. In addition, in the event that an undivided owner relinquishes his share or dies and there are no heirs, that person's share will be transferred to another undivided owner.
There are two ways to divide undivided property: 1 * Consent distribution: In principle, the division method must be done by discussion among all undivided owners. Auction and distribution of dividends and transfer of shares to an undivided owner with compensation.
If an undivided owner is entitled to a debt to another undivided owner due to maintenance, management or other burden on the undivided property, the undivided owner may repay the debtor by taking a portion of the undivided property. Of the debtor. On the other hand, in case it is necessary to sell the undisclosed property of the debtor, the creditor can claim the sale to the court.
Overall, undivided ownership is the right that multiple owners have over a single undivided property that is assumed to be in equal proportion if the owner does not clearly define it. Therefore, each owner must clearly understand his rights in each undivided part so as not to create difficulties in the event of a dispute.
