The Royal Government of Cambodia has decided to extend the New Year holiday from April 13 to 16, meaning that civil servants, the armed forces, workers and company employees will continue to work normally during the New Year. Next week. The decision to postpone the Lunar New Year holiday to curb rallies, the biggest culprit, led to the outbreak of 19 Kovid in Cambodia.
As of Saturday morning, April 11, 2020, most factory workers are still working, usually no one returns home. Workers at a number of factories in Phnom Penh videotaped their activities while working.
Through the video, they confirmed that the workers obeyed the instructions and decisions of the Royal Government and in order to prevent the spread of Kovid 19, they still work normally, do not return home. These workers continue to call on other workers to stay together to fight and prevent the spread of Kovid 19.
Heng Sour, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, told Fresh News on Saturday morning that about 95 percent of the factories still operating were still working. The other 5 percent asked for permission from the factory after they had a clear reason.
Heng Sour also called on factory workers to continue working to prevent the spread of Kovid 19 in Cambodia.
On April 9, the Ministry of Labor announced that workers who take time off during the Lunar New Year without the permission or consent of their employers will be subject to health care regulations. Separate) 14 days before returning to work will not receive wages during this period.
