This morning, the National Assembly held a meeting to discuss and approve the "Draft Law on National Emergency Management".
(Phnom Penh): "The draft law on governing the country is in a state of emergency", a new draft law in Cambodia, will be discussed and approved at the National Assembly meeting on the morning of April 10, 2020 This is it.
This morning's National Assembly meeting will be chaired by National Assembly President Samdech Ponhea Chakrei Heng Samrin. Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen also attended the meeting this morning.
Keut Rith, the new Minister of Justice, will lead his officials to defend the draft law in front of members of the National Assembly.
In addition to discussing and approving the "Draft Law on the Management of the Nation in Emergency", this morning's National Assembly meeting will decide on the validity of the mandate of Ms. Nou Sovanny, a Member of Parliament for Takeo Constituency, instead of Mr. Chea Van Det. Resigned to the post of Minister of Posts and Telecommunications.
The MPs attending this morning's meeting will also elect Loy Sophat as a member of the Agriculture, Rural Development, Environment and Water Resources Investment Planning Committee and Sou Phirin as a member of the Economic, Financial, Banking and Audit Committee.
Please note that the draft law on governing the country has 5 chapters and 11 articles. This draft law is designed in the spirit of the new Article 22 of the Cambodian Constitution, which states that "when the nation is in danger, the King shall publicly declare the nation in a state of emergency after The consensus of the Prime Minister, President of the National Assembly and President of the Senate.
Article 7 of Chapter 4 on Prosecution states that intentional obstruction or obstruction of emergency response operations is punishable by 1 to 5 years in prison and a fine of 1 million to 5 million riels. .
An act of obstruction of operation during a state of emergency is punishable by five to 10 years in prison if the act leads to public unrest or endanger national security.
Article 8 of the same chapter states that intentional acts that do not comply with the measures set forth by the Royal Government, as stated in Article 5, are punishable by imprisonment from one month to one year and a fine of 100,000 to 1 million riels. Separately, the act of non-compliance with measures when the country is in a state of emergency is punishable by 1 to 5 years in prison and a fine of 1 million to 5 million riels if this act leads to public unrest.
