In a press conference on the progress and direction of the Ministry of Information on the morning of April 9, 2020, Mr. Meas Sophorn confirmed that the Ministry of Information has officially licensed to publish through the website, not through the social network Facebook. That.
Mr. Meas Sophorn said, "The Ministry of Information gives permission to publish through the website, not through the social network Facebook, so the Ministry of Information has never considered the dissemination of information on Facebook as a recognized media entity. "By the registration law at the Ministry of Information, we only provide recognition for the registration of information disseminated through the website."
The senior official of the Ministry of Information also considered that Facebook users who are intending to spread false information must be held accountable before the law, so that the person does not publish or share information. This, of course, requires his own responsibility.
He observed that in the past, there have been some reports that deviate from the truth and are also held accountable before the law. According to Meas Sophorn, the Ministry of Information always encourages and calls on social media users to take care to share or disseminate information to the public responsibly.
He added that if the Ministry of Information deems any news that is a challenge to the society, the Ministry sends this information to the Ministry of Interior to investigate and take legal action.
He also claimed that in case of unintentional misrepresentation of journalists, the Ministry proposes an amendment to the law on journalism, but if the reporter intentionally misrepresents the information, the media will be held legally liable. The same applies.
To date, the Ministry of Information has licensed 536 media outlets, including 18 foreign publishers.
