Foreign Doctors: Dog and Cat Trafficking in Southeast Asia Continues to Rise to Nearly 10 Million a Year
FOUR PAWS warns of the serious health risks posed by uncontrolled rabies, which is just one of many diseases. Unhygienic conditions associated with the trade in dog and cat meat, combined with the risk of infection, as many different animals are caged and killed nearby, suggesting a breeding ground for new diseases. Many and deadly, such as Kovid 19 disease.
"Although most people in Southeast Asia oppose the dog and cat trade, about 10 million animals are traded annually," she said. Rising trade and livestock markets across Southeast Asia are on the verge of losing control if the governments of the countries in Southeast Asia do not take action and close these brutal markets.
During the pandemic, the World Health Organization (WHO) confirmed that 70% of the pathogens that cause disease worldwide had been detected in the last 50 years. Born of animals. Despite the public health risks, animal trafficking and restaurant owners, many dogs and cats, reported that the business was booming during the outbreak.
FOUR PAWS supports local and community animal welfare organizations with stray animal care programs and encourages Southeast Asian countries to take steps to ensure that dog and cat meat trafficking is no longer a source of disease Which is part of the protection of its people and the entire world community.
