Provincial authorities and Kampong Chhnang provincial tourism department are turning Phnom Kraing Dey Meas and Andong Russey village into natural cultural tourism sites.
(Kampong Chhnang): About 10 families of Andong Russey villagers will be modeled in the development of houses to demonstrate pot making, palm growing, palm sugar production and access to chemical-free food services. National and international tourists, including turning Kraing Dey Meas Mountain into a natural cultural tourism area. This is according to Mr. Sun Sovannarith, Deputy Governor in charge of tourism.
Mr. Sun Sovannarith told the villagers that the purpose that the provincial authorities want is for the villagers of Andong Russey to become friendly with tourists, raise chickens, ducks, grow crops and vegetables that do not use chemicals. Keep the environment around their homes good.
Continue to increase and preserve our ancestral heritage, such as pottery by hand, the production of chemical-free palm sugar, care for palm trees and planting, because the palm trees that grow in the fields are a unique spectacle in the dry season as well. The rainy season can attract foreign tourists to our house in our village as well, because in their country, they already have high-rise buildings, but they do not have as many natural cultural sites as our country.
As for Phnom Kraing Dey Meas, it is also the main goal of the provincial authorities to turn this mountain into a tourist area with Andong Russey village, because tourists can climb to the top of the mountain to see the shape and view of the whole city of Kampong Chhnang as well. The Tonle Sap area, palm plantations and large rice fields are also beautiful.
Mr. Seng Saly, Director of Kampong Chhnang Provincial Department of Tourism, said that there are already two model houses for presenting to guests, which are currently operating, but the purpose of the provincial authorities and the Department of Tourism is to expand to earn more money for the villagers. Bamboo wells to reduce their migration.
