Senior Minister Kun Kim sets up a disinfection system at O'Smach
(Oddar Meanchey): Senior Minister Kun Kim, Chairman of the National Assistance Team for Oddar Meanchey Province, on the afternoon of April 7, 2020, led technicians to prepare and install disinfection system at O'Smahn border checkpoint in Oddar Meanchey province to prevent Kovid 19 infection when Cambodians return from Thailand or guests enter and leave the port.
In addition to installing disinfection systems at the border, Senior Minister Kun Kim also distributed about 75,000 masks and 35 sets of heaters to distribute to people to prevent the spread and spread of disease. Kovid 19.
Mass aid, sprayers and disinfectants were provided by the Cambodian Emergency Association and the Hunan Chinese Chamber of Commerce.
On that occasion, the Senior Minister also called on the people to be careful to avoid infection and urged all means of transportation to reach the destination will have a telephone for communication. In particular, the doctors on duty there have to take care of and distribute some food to the people who have just returned from Thailand.
Please note that in Oddar Meanchey province, so far there are no 19 cases of Kovid. Senior Minister Kun Kim has been leading a hard-working team to arrange for the 14-day isolation of returnees from Thailand to avoid transmitting the disease to their families in the event of 19 cases of Kovid. .
In addition to activities to prevent and prevent the spread of 19 Kovid disease between people from Thailand and local people, Senior Minister Kun Kim also took action to bring food to people living in poverty and affected by Kovid. 19. The senior minister also instructed them on measures to prevent the transmission and transmission of Kovid 19.
Letter to readers: A Cambodian civil servant who recovered from Kovit 19 shared about the treatment and use of drugs and the ability of Cambodian doctors
(Phnom Penh): On April 7, 2020, a Cambodian civil servant who was cured of 19 Kovid in Phnom Penh wrote about his Kovid treatment to Fresh News. To help spread the word about what he experienced in the treatment and for the benefit of understanding and understanding to our Cambodian people.
The letter from the former Kovid patient described in full detail as follows:
As a 39-year-old civil servant, I would like to share my personal experiences and views of those who underwent 19 weeks of treatment for Kovit 19 at a Khmer-Soviet hospital.
1: Infection and early symptoms
2: Therapeutic drugs
3: Daily symptoms and disease status
4: Virus testing
At first, the doctor told me to take the test every three days, and if it was negative, put it somewhere else to be ready to receive other positive people. Later, they changed the policy to check every two days. After two negative tests in a row, he was released from the hospital and continued to be monitored for two weeks. During this two-week period, repeat the test once or twice to make sure the virus is gone.
Two consecutive negative test results are not immediate. If you look at the patients around me, including myself, it takes us about three weeks for the viruses to go away on their own, depending on the strength and immunity of our immune system and the duration of the infection. . Some may be faster or longer, meaning they cannot set a specific time.
5: Emotional management
I also asked them, "What good is it for Cambodia to keep you in the hospital if you recover from that disease?" I said that if in France just ask them to do a test, they do not do. They are also silent. I did not end the sentence that Cambodia looks after them for free and that Cambodia needs to take care of its own people.
6: Treatment building
7: Preventive measures and preparedness for major emergencies
The dedication of the medical staff on the front lines was very high. During the care of the patient, they were unable to see their family, and at the end of the shift, they were placed separately for 14 days for medical check-ups before being able to return to their families. .
Our problem today is that we lack medical staff and the treatment facilities are technically standard and hygienic. Although Soviet hospitals had high standards, the preparations were not prepared for severe cases. For example, I have a lung problem, but in the treatment room there is no lung scanner and they can not take me to another building for a lung scan. The treatment room should also have a separate building that can accommodate critically ill patients.
The medical staff does not seem to have the same skills yet. Some doctors take samples for nosebleeds, some patients are not yet proficient in taking samples through the throat, some doctors insert the wrong needle, etc. But this is just a small case. However, there should be exercises in both urban and rural areas to prepare for major emergencies that require large numbers of medical staff, including reserve personnel to protect both citizens and medical staff.
With better training, they will be more confident in working equally and the people will be more confident. The biggest concern is the technical standards and hygiene level of rural clinics. This is certainly not a simple matter and requires step-by-step scenario preparation and technical setup with detailed planning in advance.
From a Cambodian civil servant who recovered from Kovit 19 in Phnom Penh
