Until 2010, under a strategic decision by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia moved from Cambodia from an agricultural country to meet the needs of the domestic market to a country producing agricultural products for export. This is a change in the face of Cambodia in history after this poor country emerged from the flames of decades of war.
Currently, Cambodia is a country that relies on agriculture for about 34%. There are 2,818,323 hectares of wet paddy land, 534,024 hectares of dry paddy land, 25,249 hectares of mixed crop area and 47,919 hectares of industrial crop area. As a result, these crops account for 34% of the total labor force. Other sectors attracting the workforce include textiles, tourism and industry.
Due to the large size of Cambodia's agricultural sector, in 2019, Cambodia produced a total of 31 million tons of food, worth a total of 10 billion dollars. Cambodia produces a total of 7 million tons of food, valued at about $ 3 billion.
Among Cambodia's total food exports, rice is the number one exporter. In 2019, for example, Cambodia exported about 620,264 tons of rice worth about 500 million US dollars.
According to a report from the Cambodian Rice Federation in 2019, there are three types of rice that Cambodia exports, including fragrant rice, plain rice and steamed rice. The three main destinations for Cambodia's rice exports are China, the European Union and ASEAN member states.
In 2019, Cambodia received a quota of 400,000 tons of rice exports to China from the Chinese government. The huge opening of the Chinese government's rice market has made China one of Cambodia's three largest international markets. In 2019, Cambodia exported 248.105 million tons of fragrant rice to China, an increase of 33% compared to the same period in 2018.
Exports to the Chinese market account for about 40 percent of total rice exports to international markets. The large number of fragrant rice exports to China has overtaken the Chinese market to overtake the Cambodian rice market in Europe, which has always been the leading market in the past. This was confirmed by Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture and Fisheries.
2017 is the first year that Cambodia has received a quota to export 100,000 tons of rice to the Chinese market. After receiving this quota, Cambodia exported nearly 200,000 tons of rice to the People's Republic of China, exceeding the quota limit. Only the government of this long-time ally of Cambodia has increased the new quota in 2018 to 300,000 tons to export rice to the market of this country.
Unfortunately, in 2018, Cambodia exported only 170,000 tons of rice to the People's Republic of China. By 2019, Cambodia has received a quota of 400,000 tons of rice to export to China, and as a result, Cambodia can only export more than 240,000 tons of rice to this large international market.
The failure to meet the open market needs of the world's second largest economy is a matter of urgency for the relevant ministries and institutions. To meet the needs of this large international market. This was stated by Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
We are coordinating, especially the Ministry of Commerce, to set up a coordination mechanism to set up a coordination mechanism to negotiate with Cofco. Cofco is a state-owned company of the People's Republic of China, and we are working with the private sector to bring the Cambodian private sector, which intends to export rice, to work closely with Cofco. "But this is the task of the Ministry of Commerce to speed up the export of rice to China."
In addition to the added value that makes Cambodian rice sold in the Chinese market more expensive, the combination of the cost of the high-cost Cambodian rice production chain also makes Cambodian rice more expensive in the international market. . This is according to the addition of the Minister of Agriculture.
Factors that make the price of Cambodian rice expensive include transportation infrastructure problems that lead to long delays and high transportation costs. In addition, the cost of electricity and the shortage of water for cultivation are also important factors. Competitiveness with neighboring countries, including Thailand and Vietnam, is also important in international market competition.
As an iron friend, the effectiveness of the leaders of the two countries, China has made significant contributions to the development of Cambodia in general and in agriculture in particular.
In addition to opening up markets with high quotas, Chinese friends have also helped develop other sectors of the agricultural sector. This was confirmed by Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture.
Although Cambodia has not yet been able to meet the quotas provided by the Chinese side, in 2019, China has recently become the largest market for Cambodian fragrant rice. Having overtaken the European Union, which used to be Cambodia's largest rice market in the past.
It should be informed that Cambodia's major international markets at present are three, including China, Europe and ASEAN. ASEAN countries that buy the most Cambodian rice are Malaysia, Brunei and Singapore. Currently, Cambodia is considering the Philippine market more. The fastest growing market is Australia.
Leng Navatra Natural Agricultural Market is an agricultural market that serves natural and chemical-free fish, meat and vegetables. In fact, during the outbreak of Covid-19, the demand for natural fish, meat and vegetables increased significantly, according to market manager Leng Navatra.
"Currently, Leng Navatra Natural Agriculture Market has only one branch, but we plan to open 250 markets by 2020, which will keep the market close to consumers," he said. In particular, the products that are sold in the market are local products of Cambodian farmers.
