Thirty-two families in Ang Snoul district protest over private land in Thnal Toteung village, Damnak Ampil commune, with the landlord and commune authorities
(Kandal): After the explosion of facts related to the case of land next to Sala Road, a plot of land sold to the owner of Rasmei Pich Nil Club and another private person, there was a protest from all 32 families living in Thnal Toteung village, Damnak Ampil commune, Ang Snoul district. Criticizing that the landlord conspired with the villages, communes, and districts to hide information on two plots of land adjacent to Sala Road, without confirming to residents that the land was for sale.
Ms. Sen Sophie continued that when they bought the land, they did not see the owner of the plot put a split pole on the plot next to the road, which led them to understand that this is a marketing strategy to deceive people to buy land. There is a big road, but when the villagers sell it and build houses, the land next to this road turns into private property, which hurts the people because they lost to the tricks of unscrupulous traders in cooperation with the local authorities.
She said that all the villagers accused the two plots owners, Mr. Loeng Nou, a cattle buffalo, and Son, a Samrong Leu commune chief and the husband of Ms. Su Kalyan, a Damnak Ampil commune chief, for dishonesty and fraud. Their eyes that hurt them, tears, these two plots owners should not have such a business idea to hurt them at all.
However, Ms. Sen Sophy still asked the provincial governor Kong Sophoan to look into this matter, especially to see the work of subordinates who issued vague documents.
Regarding this case, Ms. Sukalyan, the chief of Damnak Ampil commune, said that the authorities have tried to solve these problems twice, with the club owner agreeing to a concession of 3 meters in addition to another 5 meters of land belonging to Phim Phorn. The road was opened for the people to come and go, and on this side, Sen Sophie and the villagers demanded that the road be the same size. He said how to open if this is private land?
Regarding the documents that the people were wondering about, the commune chief also stated that this was her fault because she signed the documents and did not check all the details because there was too much work and the documents were pushed to the district and the trust stamp was signed on the commune, but when she realized that it was written Wrong, the authorities also sent the correct letter back to Ms. Sen Sophie to end the misunderstanding.
The commune chief added that the authorities will continue to coordinate, that is, all parties can get based on the principle of win-win together, without any side.
