(Phnom Penh): After strong momentum in the last two decades, Cambodia's economic growth is expected to slow to 2.3% for 2020.
She added, "The Royal Government of Cambodia has taken the right steps to address the unprecedented 19 Kovid challenges, such as supporting the wages of garment workers, as well as reducing taxes and debts to businesses." The Royal Government is also preparing a reserve budget for the necessary interventions to reduce the impact of this crisis on the national economy, especially on vulnerable people.
In 2020, Cambodia's services sector will shrink by about 1.7% due to a deficit in tourism and a slowdown in real estate growth.
Growth in the industrial sector is projected to slow to 6.5 percent due to lower production of garments for export, as well as a slower construction sector.
Inflation is expected to remain low, averaging 2.1% in 2020, as growth slows and inflation remains low in 2021 due to lower international oil prices.
The ADO report highlights the Royal Government's commitment to diversify the financial sector beyond the banking system, as well as to establish local capital markets to provide the financing needed for diversification, investment and growth.
