Keut Rith vows to work for reform to strengthen trust in the judiciary (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Keut Rith, the new Minister of Justice, has announced his commitment to reform the law and justice system in Cambodia in line with policy. Of the Royal Government to strengthen confidence in the justice system in Cambodia.
Mr. Koeut Rith made his commitment during his inauguration ceremony, which was presided over by Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior, on April 1, 2020.
Mr. Keut Rith said that he will continue to achieve the reform achievements achieved and continue to strengthen and expand the reform achievements for the benefit of the justice sector and society and strengthen the good governance system to be the core in leading the justice sector. Such as ensuring internal unity within the leadership of the sector to participate responsibly in the cause of reform.
In addition, he will strengthen the integrity and accuracy of the performance of court officials by strengthening professional discipline in this field to further enhance the honor and dignity of the judiciary.
The senior minister, a senior law student from France, stressed that achieving these goals and commitments requires the active participation of all leaders and officials of the Ministry of Justice, as well as court officials. All levels as well as other relevant institutions.
It should be noted that Mr. Keut Rith was appointed by the Prime Minister of Cambodia Samdech Techo Hun Sen as the Minister of Justice to replace Mr. Ang Vong Vathana, who held this ministry for 16 years. Mr. Keut Rith was entrusted by the National Assembly on March 30, 2020.
Keut Rith became another young minister after Say Sam Al, the Minister of Environment for the Royal Government of Cambodia.
Mr. Keut Rith, 41 years old, was born on January 2, 1979 and was born in Prey Toteung Village, Prey Chhor District, Kampong Cham Province. He was a hard-working student, even though his family life was not very good, but he still worked hard to support his family and study hard until he received a scholarship to study law in France in 1999.
In 2000, he received a bachelor's degree in private law from the University of Lyon II, France. In 2001, he received his Maîtrise Law degree from Lyon II University with excellent grades. In 2002, he received a Master's Degree in Criminal Law and Science from the University of Lyon 3, France.
Keut Rith decided to return to Cambodia, initially as a professor of law at the Royal University of Law and Economics in both French and Khmer.
From 2002 to 2016 to share knowledge to Cambodian students and civil servants. Keut Rith is one of the founders of the Criminal Code and the Code of Criminal Procedure, among other leaders of the Ministry of Justice. During that time, Mr. Keut Rith was also the person who disseminated the law related to the Criminal Code and the Code of Criminal Procedure to the judges, prosecutors and law enforcement officers in the provinces.
