Two heads of deities and asuras were found in a ditch near the ghost gate of Angkor Thom
(Siem Reap): The two stone pillars of Preah Sisak, one angel and one asura (giant), which is a sculpture at the ghost gate of Angkor Thom, have been registered by the Inventory, Art and Heritage Police and the Travel Agency. Of APSARA Authority to extract from the canal east of the ghost gate on April 1, 2020.
Mr. Chhouk Somala, an expert in inventory of the Art Department of the Department of Temple Preservation in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology, said that the characteristics of the statue of Sisak Tevda were found in four pieces: the whole Sisak was broken to the mouth and the hair was damaged. Nostrils protrude from left and right earlobe fragments. Asuras, on the other hand, has only the back with hair ornaments and no face.
The discovery of the two Sisak statues in the canal was not accidental, according to Somala, as it was due to the general structure of the structure being damaged by the age of the building, natural factors and The war has caused the statues to fall into the water, some buried in the ground, some on the ground.
After the handover, the two statues were handed over to the APSARA Authority for temporary storage to register the art objects and will be repaired before being handed over to the museum.
