Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, presides over a meeting of the Standing Committee of the Council of Ministers to review the draft law on emergency management (Video inside) (Phnom Penh). Meeting of the Standing Committee of the Council of Ministers to review the draft law governing the state of emergency.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen at a press conference on March 30 yesterday announced the decision to draft a law governing the country in a state of emergency. Samdech Techo Hun Sen confirmed that Cambodia does not yet have the law to govern the country in times of emergency. Therefore, the creation of this law to be a legal tool to manage the country in an emergency.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated, "We have already drafted this law, including the statement. "We will have a division of what to do, what not to do, and then put in place measures to close any public and private places that are necessary to respond."
Samdech Techo confirmed that the draft law governing the country in times of emergency was prepared by Mr. Keut Rith, the new Minister of Justice, and sent to all Deputy Prime Ministers.
According to the Cambodian Constitution, declaring a country in a state of emergency is an application of Article 22 when the nation is in unpredictable danger. However, the Cambodian Constitution does not clearly state the meaning or actions of placing the country in a state of emergency.
The new Article 22 states that "When the nation is in danger, the King shall publicly declare the nation in a state of emergency after the approval of the Prime Minister, the President of the National Assembly and the President of the Senate."
Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that after reviewing at the meeting of the Standing Committee of the Council of Ministers this morning, the draft law on emergency management will be discussed and decided at the plenary meeting of the Council of Ministers on Friday. This week.
