Air pollution levels in Europe are declining significantly
(Brussels): Air pollution levels in major cities across Europe have dropped significantly as many European countries are implementing measures to shut down their countries in whole or in part to reduce the rate of transmission. COVID-19. But experts warn that the city's population remains the most vulnerable to the pandemic. This was released by CNA on Monday morning, March 30, 2020.
The decline is due to European countries taking measures to close the whole country or part of it, as well as restricting the activities of the people. In Madrid, for example, from March 16 to 22, nitrogen dioxide levels fell 56 percent from week to week after the Spanish government declared a state of emergency, banning insignificant travel last year. March 14
Air pollution, especially nitrogen dioxide, can cause lung cancer, tuberculosis and stroke, and according to the European Environment Agency (EEA), about 400,000 people are affected. Die prematurely "every year across Europe.
