To help combat the disruption of children's education and ensure that children continue to study safely, UNICEF has allocated additional funding to accelerate work with governments and partners in more than 145 low- and middle-income countries. .
A global initial budget allocation of $ 13 million, of which nearly $ 9 million from the Global Partnership for Education (GPE) contribution - will be used as a catalyst through government-level support. Nations and educational partners in each country plan to be able to respond more quickly across the entire system.
The project will enable countries to develop curriculum options in the event of school closures and enable schools to continue to keep children and their communities safe by providing important information on hand washing and hygiene practices. Other.
The fund will also support children's mental health and prevent stigma and discrimination by encouraging students to avoid negative attitudes when talking about the virus.
In Cambodia, UNICEF is supporting the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports to provide alternative study opportunities for students, such as electronic classes on key subjects.
The program can cover and benefit about 250,000 ninth and 12th grade students across the country who will be taking the exam this year. Online classes will support more students to study during the school holidays due to the 19 19 outbreak of Kovid.
UNICEF is also working with the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports to expand its ongoing program to provide alternative learning services to students at the elementary, secondary and primary levels.
With the support of the Government of Japan, many e-learning lessons and programs have been developed and disseminated through the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports' website and Facebook page.
Outreach materials focusing on Kovid 19 prevention education are also produced to promote the importance of hand washing and personal care to children and families and to encourage parents to support their children's learning at home. And to their general well-being and mental health.
These educational materials are available on the UNICEF and Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports websites.
In addition, with financial support from the United States Agency for International Development (USAID), sanitation facilities are being provided to 813 schools nationwide, benefiting 390,000 children. Globally, in all 145 countries, UNICEF will partner with:
1. Support the implementation of government crisis response plans such as technical assistance, rapid risk analysis, data collection and planning for school reopening.
2: Support the planning and implementation of safe school operations and risk education, such as translation, publication and implementation of guidelines on safe schools, providing schools with hygiene kits and disseminating important information. About disease prevention and training of teachers and parents, psychosocial support and mental health for themselves and for students.
3. Ensure the continuation of learning and access to distance learning programs, such as the design and preparation of alternative educational programs online, radio and television.
4. Promote knowledge sharing and capacity building for responding to current crises and possible future epidemics.
