Kovid 19 is a disease that has killed more than 20,000 people worldwide, including doctors. This disease scares people and always tries to avoid it. Do doctors, health workers and medical students who volunteer to fight this disease are not afraid?
Seng Srey Vin, a third-year student at the University of Health Sciences, said she was committed to participating in the mission. But her family and relatives did not enjoy the activity, as they feared for her safety.
Ms. Srey Vin, a student who received a scholarship to support the study of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, stated, "At first, his parents were worried about this campaign, they were afraid of contact with infected people, and we did not have specific protection, it could be easy. "The virus infects us personally, he is afraid to affect my own health."
However, because of her struggle for this important mission for the society, Srey Vin tried to explain the reasons to her family and also got permission to participate in this mission.
Another student who graduated in nursing from Thailand, Teav Sothearoth, had the same problem as Srey Vin. He was prevented from participating in the mission by his family for fear of endangering his life.
"Actually, his parents are worried," said the Thai medical student. But he believed in me and I explained to him not to be upset because the relevant ministry, the Ministry of Health, he had training before we went to fight with Kovid 19. He also encouraged and wished success.
Even though everyone knows that their life expectancy cannot last forever, when it comes to life-threatening things, they are always afraid. Participating in the Battle of Kovid 19, which can endanger health and life, was also a difficult decision. What are the main reasons why doctors, health workers and medical students volunteer to participate in this activity with courage?
Ms. Seng Srey Vin explained why she participated in this mission: "I am a student of health, so the spirit of a doctor must be involved in improving our health. Especially at a time when our country is experiencing a severe epidemic of Kovid 19, I as a doctor must attend. "On the other hand, as a people, we must join the fight against the disease."
Ms. Masakina, a Cambodian Muslim who is also studying medicine, sees that the fight against disease is a duty and duty of a doctor. She added that if the country faces disease and doctors do not do their duty, what should people expect?
Volunteering to fight the battle of Kovid 19, a dreaded disease for the common people, led to the astonishment of these volunteer doctors. Their courageous actions are considered a respectable epic for the nation and the people of Cambodia.
The doctors who participated in this mission were proud of their mission and even though it affected their lives, they were still happy because it was a mission for the nation and the people.
"I'm happy to do what I love," she said. "Even if there is something wrong with my life, I am happy because it is a sacrifice for society."
Teav Sothearoth is just like Miss Masakina. Mr. Sothearoth claimed that participating in this mission was a source of pride for him and that he was happy despite the danger to his life.
Sothearoth stated, "I said once with my family, do not regret this, because this is a skill that I love. In general, I give up, no matter what I have in this situation, I give up, I do not regret for the people, for our country.
The team of doctors who volunteered for the Battle of Kovid 19 will work directly with Kovid 19 patients in Cambodia. They will be divided into tasks assigned by the Ministry of Health team, such as therapists, samplers, caregivers and transporters.
Prior to the mission, all of these doctors will be trained and trained by experts.
The Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, during a meeting with these volunteer doctors on March 25, 2020, praised and appreciated their decision as well as their guardians.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen asked parents not to worry about the safety of children who participate in this mission, the Royal Government, especially the Ministry of Health, pay close attention to their safety. In addition, before the action is to be prepared to wear the most protective clothing according to the technique.
Samdech Techo claimed that without proper clothing, he would not be allowed to do so.
In order to encourage volunteer doctors in this important mission of the motherland, Samdech Techo Hun Sen donated 40,000 Riels per day to them for 100 days.
