(Phnom Penh): Responding to people's concerns about Cambodia's food security, including rice, vegetables, fish, meat at the time of the outbreak of Kovid 19, whether to meet the needs of the people as the disease spreads According to Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture of Cambodia, Cambodia's food supply is stable and can meet the needs of the people.
Minister Veng Sakhon stated, "As for food security for Cambodia, I do not think there will be any problems because we have a lot of rice left. Our pig farming can also supply up to 80 percent of the market. We are also operating normally, the cultivation of vegetables by farmers is also operating normally, in general, Cambodia's agricultural sector is operating normally.
At the same time, the Minister highlighted some achievements in maintaining food security achieved by the Ministry of Agriculture in 2019, such as rice cultivation across the country reached 3.33 million hectares. The average rice yield in both dry and wet season reached 3.33 tons per hectare, with a total rice yield of more than 10.88 million tons, with a surplus of rice (excess rice) of more than 5.76 million tons, equivalent to rice. And 3.69 million tons.
In particular, for the production of mixed crops and short-term industry, the yield will be more than 16 million tons in 2019. Total agricultural exports reached about 7 million tons, of which 4.87 million tons were official exports, an increase of 13% over the previous year, including bananas, cassava flour, cashew nuts, corn, rubber, sugar cane oil. Raw coconuts have increased significantly. The official export volume of rice was 620,000 tons in 2019, compared to 626 thousand tons in 2018.
According to research, in 2019, unofficial rice exports amounted to more than 2 million tons through the main ports in the provinces bordering Vietnam.
Regarding animal husbandry, the Minister stated that the Ministry has also promoted modern animal husbandry, medium and large-scale animal feed production to support the animal husbandry movement, along with the improvement of technical service system to improve animal breeding and processing. Livestock products, establishment of modern slaughterhouses, as well as facilitation of animal trade and animal products.
It is noteworthy that in the past, traditional animal husbandry has been gradually shifted from shifting production for domestic consumption to commercial breeding. This change in production style has increased steadily. In 2019, animal production reached 45.8 million head, an increase of 3.3% compared to 2018.
Commercial animal husbandry increased by 37% compared to 2018. Meat production and supply are increasing every year, from 209 thousand tons in 2015 to 239 thousand tons in 2019.
