Japan continues to provide nearly $ 400,000 grant for the construction of school buildings and water supply systems to rural schools in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Japan has provided US $ 398,113 to four Cambodian units for the construction of school buildings and water supply systems for some schools in rural Cambodia under a grant for small-scale projects. Human Security (Kusanoni Assistance).
The signing ceremony was held on March 19, 2020, signed by Mr. Mikami Masahiro, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of Japan to the Kingdom of Cambodia and representatives of the four recipients. The following projects:
* First: Water supply system construction project in Reang Khvav village, Pursat province with a budget of 103,885 USD to Dignified Life Organization. Under this project, a water supply system will be constructed and a 17-kilometer water distribution network, along with seven water distribution points, will be installed in Reang Khvav village. Through this project, more than 880 people will have access to clean water, and this project will contribute to improving their livelihoods.
Second: The project to build a school building at Krouch Primary School in O'Chum District, Ratanakkiri Province with a budget of $ 90,128 to the Department of Education, Youth and Sports of Ratanakkiri Province.
In this project, a school building with 5 rooms equipped with furniture and 4 toilets will be built at Kroch Primary School in Ratanakkiri province. It is expected that more than 320 teachers and students will benefit by improving the learning environment.
Third: The construction of a school building at Bok Teuk Primary School, Prek Prosop District, Kratie Province with a budget of $ 98,100, the grant unit of the Department of Education, Youth and Sports of Kratie Province
In this project, a school building with 5 rooms equipped with furniture and 4 toilets will be built at Bok Teuk Primary School in Kratie province. It is expected that more than 320 teachers and students will benefit by improving the learning environment.
And 4: Project to build a school building at Memang Primary School with a budget of 106,000 USD to the Department of Education, Youth and Sports of Mondulkiri Province.
In this project, a school building with 5 rooms equipped with furniture and 2 toilets will be built at Memang Secondary School, Mondulkiri Province. Expecting more than 200 teachers and students to benefit by improving the learning environment.
Japan's Kusanoni Grant began in Cambodia in 1991 to support Cambodia's efforts to develop and rebuild the country at the grassroots level. This assistance aims to protect vulnerable people from a number of common factors, such as: Poverty and misfortune, which can directly threaten their livelihoods, livelihoods and dignity, as well as promote their self-reliance. Communities.
Since 1991, the Japanese government has provided more than $ 60 million to local authorities and NGOs to implement 624 Kusano projects across Cambodia. Battambang Airport to reopen
