EU Delegation, French Development Agency Brings Electricity and Clean Water to Rural Cambodians
(Kampong Cham): Ms. Phanny, a resident of Tuol village, Batheay commune, Kampong Cham province, has expanded her stall as the electricity distribution network has now reached her village.
She explains that she not only provides better services, but also lower operating costs, and more importantly, it makes her life easier.
Ms. Panny explains the importance of electricity for her business, that her shop uses two or three refrigerators to store drinks and ice. He added that although he owed a little more if he used a lot of electricity, but still enough because of electricity, he was able to expand his business. In addition, people who have access to electricity and clean water feel that their lives are happier and fresher.
People without clean water and electricity have no chance of development. The EU addresses this challenge by supporting a unique public-private partnership model that enables the banking sector to lend to rural energy companies and small water suppliers in Cambodia by helping them expand. Ability to provide clean water and electricity to more families.
Clement Baker, project manager for the EU Delegation to Cambodia, said a $ 10 million loan had been provided to local water and electricity distribution companies to expand or increase the capacity of their networks and supply infrastructure. 50,000 families or 200,000 people have access to clean water and electricity. We are very happy to be able to improve the lives of rural people in this way.
"Our services make people feel more civilized, we do not have to be jealous of the people of the city ... In terms of price, our customers pay cheaper than the people of the city." This was confirmed by Mr. Phan Vorn, a rural water supply entrepreneur who brought a clean water distribution network to people in rural areas in Kampong Chhnang province, Cambodia.
Mr. Sokun added that the expansion requires long-term investment with out-of-pocket money. In this sense, the large size of the loan, the interest rate and the loan term really help a lot. He says his experience with other banks, loan size is based only on real estate prices. In the past, it was very difficult to have a headache when he talked to suppliers to buy equipment to expand and increase capacity by paying in installments.
As a result of such projects, more than 20,000 households have access to electricity, and more than 30,000 households, including poor households, are guaranteed to be connected to a reliable and affordable water supply network in rural Cambodia. That is due to the financial support from the EU to this project. This initiative benefits all partners, especially rural users.
