The National Bank of Cambodia (NBC) has introduced three additional measures to increase capital for banks and microfinance institutions to be able to continue lending.
(Phnom Penh): The National Bank of Cambodia (NBC) has introduced three additional measures to help banking and microfinance institutions increase their capital and working capital, continue to lend to the public, and consider other benefits for clients. Affected.
1. The National Bank of Cambodia postpones the implementation of the Prakas on the capital fund, which plans to increase the working capital of banking and financial institutions from 50% to 100% at the beginning of 2020 and postpones it to 2021. This is to make it easier for banks and microfinance institutions to have more capital in hand to lend to customers.
2. Reduce the interest rate of the liquidity guarantee operation, called LPCO. From today, the National Bank of Cambodia decided to reduce interest rates so that banking and microfinance institutions can bid for Riel to use as capital.
3: Reduce the interest rate of tradable securities (NCD), which in the past, banking and microfinance institutions bought from the National Bank of Cambodia to earn interest rates, but from tomorrow is to reduce interest rates for banking and microfinance institutions. Decrease from buying NCD to lending back to customers.
Ms. Chea Serey stated, "Observing the recent developments, we also decided to introduce three new measures to facilitate liquidity (with a lot of capital in hand). The fact that we require banking and financial institutions to continue to provide credit, or that we want institutions to remain flexible in waiving interest or principal, requires that the institution must have a lot of money in hand, which means a lot of liquidity. In the body.
Answering the question: At a time when the Cambodian economy is struggling with the Kovid 19 virus, making it difficult for business owners and people who use bank loans to repay, the National Bank of Cambodia has such a mechanism. To help them? Ms. Chea Serey said that the National Bank of Cambodia has recently introduced a mechanism to allow banks and microfinance institutions to be more lenient with customers who face repayment issues.
"The current situation has caused some businesses to face problems due to lack of customers, lower income or loss. In the event that those traders have credit with MFIs, we ask MFIs to remain flexible," she said. "According to the actual situation, being able to check the possibility of easing or delaying the raising of interest or interest."
"We have made it possible for each institution to comply with certain conditions, meaning that if the trader has a specific problem due to Kovid, it is possible that the institution may delay the payment of principal, interest or he may reduce the rate," she added. Interest, etc. according to the actual situation. "If some companies can afford to delay or repay the principal, but take these interest rates, the National Bank has requested the banking and microfinance institutions."
First, to continue to provide more active credit to priority sectors to ensure the sustainability of those businesses
Second, maintain flexibility as far as possible by examining the possibility of easing and delaying the repayment of principal and interest, especially for business owners in the hotel and guesthouse and tourism-related sectors.
Third, continue to monitor and introduce measures to prevent the spread of Covid-19 virus to employees and customers to ensure the sustainable operation of banking and financial institutions on a regular basis.
