Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan urges authorities to enforce the law on drug offenses without exception
(Phnom Penh): Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan, President of the National Authority for Combating Drugs, requested all law enforcement authorities to be very strict with drug traffickers, especially those who commit serious crimes must be prosecuted. All this without exception to give confidence to the people.
Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan's proposal comes after some public opinion criticized the authorities for applying the law to drug traffickers as unequal before the law.
Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan made this proposal on the morning of March 16, 2020, when he convened a meeting to review the work of drug control in 2019 and a meeting to review the results of the implementation of the plan. The 4th Anti-Illegal Drug Campaign at the Ministry of Interior with the participation of ministers and institutions involved in the anti-illegal drug campaign from all capitals and provinces.
Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan added that there was an increase in law enforcement, not only in the number of criminal cases cracked down, but also in the number of suspects arrested and the amount of drugs seized. Up too.
At the same time, Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan also called on relevant ministries and institutions to train trainers to participate in disseminating the dangers of drug use to people in communities throughout Cambodia.
Farmers' dryland rice in Koh Thkov commune, Chulkiri district, about 1,000 hectares no longer wither
(Kampong Chhnang): A canal more than 3 km long from Tonle Sap to Sre Prang area in Kor Chrum village, Koh Thkov commune, Chulkiri district, Kampong Chhnang province is now being rented by farmers to dig deeper and widen. According to the plan of the district authority, which received funding from the Royal Government through the Ministry and the Department of Water Resources to irrigate more than 1,000 hectares of farmland.
According to the project to dig and restore the canal, the back of the canal will also be cleared and compacted to make way for transporting agricultural products from the fields to the local area.
This move makes dryland rice farmers happy and hope that in the coming years their paddy fields will no longer lack water like in previous years and have a better life than in previous years because But they can farm twice, so the farm they have 1 hectare becomes 2 hectares, if there are 2 hectares becomes 4 hectares due to the abundance of water, they can farm twice a season.
Farmers' representatives and the water management committee in Kor Chrum village said that in this area, every year, dry farming is short of water because the old canal is shallow and farmers can not restore.
Every year, some hectares of rice paddies are damaged and many hectares of sugarcane are damaged as the weather changes. But in 2020, at the request of farmers, the head of the Royal Government has reduced the budget to help restore this canal, which is 3,500 meters deep and bigger to bring water from the river to irrigate more than 1,000 hectares of farmers. The back of the canal has also become an easy way to transport rice to their base.
The farmers congratulated and thanked the head of the Royal Government, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia and the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology, who always consider and pay attention to solve the difficulties of farmers everywhere. Especially for the lack of irrigation water.
According to the farmers, the bottom of the canal is 3 meters wide, the mouth is 12 meters wide, 9 meters deep and the total length is 3,500 meters, and to date, more than 40% of the canal has been rehabilitated. The project will be completed in mid-April.
