Apsara Authority clarifies the issue of 126 former workers working at the site of West Mebon Temple
Separately, APSARA Authority is only responsible for coordinating and resolving policies on construction and land ownership issues that may affect and facilitate the work of technical officers and foreign experts, as well as to inspect the site.
APSARA Authority said that in the process of managing the workers of the western Mebon temple site, the French School of the Far East signed a joint agreement with the International Federation of Construction and Forestry Workers in 2013 to protect workers' rights in accordance with the private sector labor law. Officially informed to APSARA Authority.
After this, in early 2018, the French School of the Far East informed the Mebon trade union about the decision to suspend the repair project due to the financial crisis of the French Ministry of European Affairs and Foreign Affairs.
Through this, the French Embassy handed over all the equipment and responsibilities of the Western Mebon site to the APSARA Authority on May 3, 2018.
After receiving the transfer, the administrative team and the specialized department of APSARA Authority visited the site with EFEO representatives, union representatives and all workers to find a solution by requesting to return to work and after explaining the management principles. New in accordance with the law on public functions and public institutions, as well as salaries and benefits received.
In response to the request of the APSARA Authority, the 126 unions and workers decided to refuse to work for the APSARA Authority due to lower wages and benefits such as the private sector labor law, and decided to seek outside employment.
As a result, the APSARA Authority decided to recruit 112 weekly workers to complete the repair work using the national budget and under the full control of the APSARA Authority, which is not related to the French School of the Far East. No more.
