Wanting to promote this national identity product, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, once said that in the phase of the spread of this new type of respiratory disease or Covid-19, if people do not wear masks, they can also wear a krama. Which is a Khmer identity inherited from ancestors to wear to cover the mouth and nose instead.
In the pre-Angkorian and Angkorian periods, the krama was also folded like a leash for the army to wear on the battlefield to fight against enemies defending the territory. In the past, the parents used the krama as a hammock to put the baby to sleep or instead of many other uses.
In general, whether wet or dry, the Khmer krama really has so many benefits that other nations besides some Southeast Asian nations do not have, and have heard of the admiration of other nations over the idea of creating this krama. Cambodians, as some of them, have shown their love and use of our popular Khmer krama.
In ancient times, krama was used to fight beasts and fight enemies, but in modern times, krama has been used as a mask to fight nose and throat infections, especially Covid-19.
The widespread use of Khmer krama throughout the country thus presents a meaningful aspect for the Khmer nation and inspires the love and preservation of the ancestral heritage identity. There is something even more special than this: the raising of hands, saluting, bowing to each other for the Cambodian people.
What the people are asking for is to call on the traders who sell the krama not to raise the price like in the case of the masses, because this handicraft product can be produced in our own country, which is also an expression of etiquette. Morality, understanding, helping each other is a tradition of our Khmer nation.
