Therefore, in order to prevent and control the above crimes, the "Law on Anti-Money Laundering and Terrorist Financing", along with a number of relevant provisions, was enacted with the aim of launching measures to combat money laundering and Organizing, managing and monitoring the implementation of those measures.
To better understand the definition of money laundering and measures to control this fraudulent activity, Fresh News' Legal Awareness Program, in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, raises the topic of "Anti-Money Laundering Measures". Explain and share to all citizens, students as follows:
Pursuant to the "Anti-Money Laundering Law and the Financing of Terrorism" dated June 24, 2007, the activities considered as money laundering include the following:
In real society, for example, we see some individuals concealing their illicit assets by investing in real estate, depositing in banks, setting up a money exchange business, investing in securities, using gambling establishments. Other and business activities related to the organization, foundation, etc.
As a precautionary measure, the Anti-Money Laundering and Terrorism Financing Act stipulates the obligations of relevant institutions, such as banks, financial institutions, microfinance agencies, real estate agents, post offices, casinos, foundations, foundations, foundations and other professional organizations. Guidance by the Financial Intelligence Organization.
The obligations that the relevant institutions (reporting persons) must perform include noticing any large cash transactions (cash in excess of R40,000,000) and prohibiting any unidentified bank account. Identify the identity of the customer and the staff, monitor and monitor any transaction or customer that they suspect may be involved in money laundering.
After receiving any irregular or suspicious information, the reporting person shall submit a report to the Financial Intelligence Organization for follow-up and further action. However, the reporting person must also keep all relevant information confidential.
To ensure that obliged persons report and perform their duties consistently, the above law imposes penalties on non-compliant persons, such as warning, reprimanding, revocation of business license, prohibition of any transaction. Fines and even imprisonment can result.
At the same time, individuals who are accused of committing money laundering offenses will be detained on the property involved or suspected of being involved in the offense without any transfer movement until a final decision is made. Court.
In summary, the reporting persons and relevant institutions should properly perform all their duties and responsibilities in accordance with the law in order to avoid penalties as well as jointly prevent "money laundering" which is illegal in Society, too.
