The warning comes after the Ministry of Information recently observed that a number of individuals in Cambodia using Facebook and online have used the name of the media to spread false, unprofessional and immoral information. Names of other professional journalists.
Mr. Meas Sophorn stated that "the Ministry of Information will work with relevant institutions to take legal action in force in the Kingdom of Cambodia, as we have done in the past and related to the dissemination of false information on some websites." .
Meas Sophorn, a spokesman for the Ministry of Information, confirmed: "As is the case, we have given him the final condition that he make a written contract and that he still violates the contract, does not comply with professional ethics, as well as "The law on the press regime, the Ministry will consider the termination of his license and can proceed with the legal process in force."
Mr. Pen Bona, President of the Club of Cambodian Journalists, supported the Ministry of Information's move to call individuals who pretend to be journalists and act inappropriately in journalism and away from the profession to educate now, however. There should be stricter measures.
"If so, I support stricter measures to ensure the character of journalists, as the Prime Minister said, 'Do not violate the rights of others, do not distort the truth,' so we are journalists," he said. "We have to report honestly, professionally and ethically. We are not an authority, we are not a court, we do not have the right to intimidate others. This is not the nature of the press."
The Law on the Press, promulgated in 1995, requires journalists to adhere to a number of ethics, such as publishing news in good faith and commenting or criticizing it in a fair and just manner, using honest methods to find news, photos and videos. Documents must respect the rights and freedoms of individuals in private life.
The law on the press does not set a level of education or experience for the media business. This law was supported by the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, to amend it. In the past, some opinions have been required to determine the level of education or experience for publishers, while others have suggested that if this level of education is limited, it should not affect the right to freedom of expression.
