(Kampong Chhnang): The Provincial Gendarmerie, led by Mr. Tim Nin, Deputy Prosecutor of Kampong Chhnang Provincial Court, on the afternoon of March 12, 2020, stopped a machine that was stealing land in Dambok Village. Koh Koh, Svay Chrum commune, Rolea Bier district, without permission from the land owner. The seizure of the above machinery is based on the complaint of the land owner, who also filed a complaint to the Prosecutor of the Kampong Chhnang Provincial Court.
According to the information from the land owner who filed the complaint to the court, he was identified as Min Puth Chetthanvann, 51, a health official living in Phnom Penh, who had bought a paddy field. Hectares at Boeung Tamrek in Dambok Kak Koh village, Svay Chrum commune, Rolea Bier district during 2016 and has been occupied until now.
Suddenly, in mid-February 2020, Bui Minh Tam, a 32-year-old Vietnamese man using a Cambodian identity card, unloaded several machines to dig up his land without permission, despite his ban. This has never been heard of. Later, he filed a complaint to Svay Chrum commune chief to help solve the problem.
Due to this, he filed a complaint to the court for legal action, demanding 200 million riels in damages, demanding that the name be filled with earth, clearing the pit and asking the court to enforce the law.
At the same time, the deputy prosecutor detained a chief for questioning as to who ordered the excavation of his land without such permission.
Please be reminded that Buoy Min Tang bought land in that area to develop people living on floating houses according to the provincial policy, but this name has been using excavators to remove debris from an ancient lake. In two areas, Boeung Tamrek, which has been excavated for many hectares to fill the flooded forest in the two areas in the picture turned into residential land, with each pit being Dig a depth of 4 to 5 meters.
The name Buoy Minh Tam is also being claimed by another villager who bought only 37 hectares of land from him, but took up to 40 hectares of his land, and now the case is in court. Also.
