(Phnom Penh): The General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia (ACC) in collaboration with the Anti-Corruption Unit (ACU) organized a lecture on the “Anti-Corruption Law” under the chairmanship of Mr. Kheang Seng, Deputy Director of the Anti-Corruption Unit, on behalf of Dr. Senior Minister Om. Yentieng, Chairman of the Anti-Corruption Unit, with the participation of all levels of the AOC on March 12, 2020.
During the ceremony, Dr. Kun Nhem, Royal Government Delegate in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia, also highlighted the progress of governance, trade facilitation and successful implementation of the Customs Administration Reform and Modernization Program in the past. In particular, the results have been remarkable in the last five years, with an average revenue growth of 19.5% per year.
In fact, in 2019, the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia collected more than 42% of taxes and duties, and compared to 2014, the tax revenue achieved by the General Department of Customs and Excise in 2019 more than doubled from 5.3 trillion riels to 13 trillion riels or from about 1,318.8 million US dollars to 3,215.2 million US dollars.
In this lecture, Mr. Kheang Seng mentioned the main purpose of the Anti-Corruption Law to prevent and prevent everyone from committing corruption through the three mottos: "Do not want, can not and do not dare to commit corruption."
He then presented the history of the Anti-Corruption Unit, the Anti-Corruption Institution, the investigation power, the types of corruption and how to file a complaint.
The Anti-Corruption Unit has been carrying out its work supported by the Anti-Corruption Law, the Code of Criminal Procedure and the Criminal Code, and the Law on the Amendment of the Anti-Corruption Law.
This presentation highlights the types of corruption crimes that public officials need to pay attention to: C-bribery, bribery, bribery, business, passive influence, and abuse of power. Illegal and proceeds of corruption.
Overall, the Anti-Corruption Unit has been playing an important role in promoting governance and the rule of law, as well as participating in and acting as a mirror to reflect the performance of public institutions and stakeholders to improve operational services and efficiency. Perform its duties and responsibilities in a transparent, honest, in accordance with the laws and regulations in force.
The current presentation on the Anti-Corruption Law has increased the knowledge of Customs and Excise officials at all levels on the content of corruption and its consequences and has become a driving force for customs officials. And all IRS take care to carry out their daily work with professional skills and professional ethics to strengthen the collection of tax revenue into the national budget more effectively.
