(Phnom Penh): Mr. Khuong Sreng, Governor of Phnom Penh, this morning, March 13, 2020, ordered the administration of 14 districts to deliver water to people who face daily shortages, especially people living in the suburbs to Temporary supply for the dry season.
In a meeting with officials related to the lack of clean water in Phnom Penh, Mr. Khuong Sreng said that Phnom Penh today not only expanded in size, but also increased in height with many high-rise housing, so the need for water. Many are also on the rise, leading to shortages.
At the same time, Mr. Khuong Sreng also asked for the highest level of tolerance and understanding from the people, especially those living on the outskirts of Phnom Penh who face the problem of lack of clean water. However, he called on people living in the center of Phnom Penh to save water use to create a full flow of water in the hose that shoots from the production tank.
Please be informed that the problem of lack of clean water for the residents of Phnom Penh, especially in the suburbs is facing every day, so some areas require the authorities and the authorities to cooperate to transport water to supply regularly to facilitate. Temporary shortages of people this dry season.
Mr. Sim Sitha, Director General of Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority, said that every day in Phnom Penh produces more than 640,000 cubic meters of clean water, while the demand has increased to more than 700,000 cubic meters, causing people Living in Phnom Penh lacks about 100,000 cubic meters of water for daily use.
Mr. Sim Sitha said that the water production of Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority has not decreased, that is, the capacity to produce clean water is increasing every year, but because the demand is higher than expected. He said that in 2016, it produced only more than 400,000 cubic meters, while currently producing more than 600,000 cubic meters per day.
In response to the increasing demand for clean water from the people from year to year, in early 2020, the Royal Government of Cambodia has built a new water treatment plant in Sangkat Bak Khe, Khan Chroy Changva, Phnom Penh. Using a budget of more than 300 million US dollars, which has the capacity to supply nearly 400,000 cubic meters of water per day.
Regarding this case, Mr. Sim Sitha claimed that according to the plan, the water treatment plant will be completed in 2023, at which time the capacity to produce clean water will increase further, so he ensures that the water supply to the people. Throughout Phnom Penh, there will be no shortage.
According to a statement read by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the second protocol on the revision of the ASEAN Comprehensive Investment Agreement was signed in Hanoi, Vietnam on September 21, 2017 by Mr. Pan Sosak. The Minister of Commerce and the Minister of Economy of ASEAN represent the Royal Government of Cambodia, along with government representatives of other ASEAN Member States.
The Protocol aims to establish a free and open investment regime in ASEAN to achieve the ultimate goal of economic integration under the ASEAN Economic Community. This Protocol is composed of three articles, including Article 1: Amendments to the definition of "physical person", Article 2: Provision for revision, Article 7 (prohibition on obligations) and Article 3: Provisions on the final provisions.
The second protocol amends the ASEAN Comprehensive Investment Agreement, hereinafter referred to as the following two points:
In this regard, the copyright continues to apply only to ASEAN Member States that do not yet have a residency regime, but any ASEAN Member State that already has a residency regime and must provide the benefits of this Agreement to the residency to other ASEAN Member States in principle. Reaction. The new definition of the protocol allows other countries, such as Cambodia, to benefit from this agreement to also resident investors in the future, if these countries have a resident regime.
